**assistant**:
> The two biggest causes are likely:
> 
> 1. **Your browser fallback is broken / timing out on most ordinary pages.**
> 2. **Your bundled Pyppeteer Chromium is probably genuinely ancient, while your user agent claims Chrome 91—which is itself now ancient.**
> 
> More broadly, I would make PDF finding a primarily **metadata/API-driven retrieval problem**, with browser automation only as a last resort.
> 
> ## 1. Important bug: `fetch_url()` waits for a *second* navigation
> 
> `page.goto()` already waits for navigation. This line then waits for another navigation event that usually never occurs:
> 
> ```python
> await page.waitForNavigation({'timeout': 10000})
> ```
> 
> So a normal successfully loaded page will commonly fail after ten seconds.
> 
> Replace `fetch_url` with:
> 
> ```python
> async def fetch_url(page, url):
>     response = await page.goto(
>         url,
>         {
>             'timeout': 30000,
>             'waitUntil': 'domcontentloaded',
>         },
>     )
> 
>     if response is None:
>         raise RuntimeError(f"No response returned for {url}")
> 
>     # Optionally allow JS-rendered content a moment to settle.
>     await asyncio.sleep(0.5)
> 
>     return await page.content(), response.headers.get('content-type', '')
> ```
> 
> If a site needs more rendering, prefer a specific selector:
> 
> ```python
> await page.waitForSelector("article, main, body", {"timeout": 10000})
> ```
> 
> rather than waiting for an unspecified next navigation.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. The “browser is outdated” error is probably real
> 
> Pyppeteer is old and historically downloads a very old Chromium build. A user-agent override does not change:
> 
> - JavaScript/browser feature support;
> - TLS and HTTP behavior;
> - Client Hints;
> - browser fingerprint;
> - Chromium’s actual rendering engine.
> 
> Moreover, this UA is now very old:
> 
> ```python
> Chrome/91.0.4472.124
> ```
> 
> ### Recommendation: use Playwright with current Chrome
> 
> Playwright is much better maintained than Pyppeteer.
> 
> ```bash
> pip install playwright
> playwright install chromium
> ```
> 
> Or, ideally, use your locally installed Chrome channel:
> 
> ```python
> from playwright.async_api import async_playwright
> 
> async with async_playwright() as p:
>     browser = await p.chromium.launch(
>         channel="chrome",  # Uses installed Google Chrome, if available
>         headless=True,
>     )
> 
>     context = await browser.new_context()
>     page = await context.new_page()
> 
>     response = await page.goto(
>         url,
>         wait_until="domcontentloaded",
>         timeout=30000,
>     )
> ```
> 
> I would **not set a spoofed user agent by default**. Let the actual browser report its own contemporary UA. If you do set one, it should match the browser reasonably closely.
> 
> Some publisher sites will still show an “outdated browser” or anti-bot page, especially with headless browsers. That is a signal to move on to another source—repository, author manuscript, PMC, arXiv, institutional archive—not to make your browser impersonation more elaborate.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Avoid `HEAD` before downloading
> 
> This is a major source of false failures.
> 
> Many academic sites:
> 
> - disallow `HEAD` but permit `GET`;
> - redirect differently for `HEAD`;
> - omit `Content-Length`;
> - report a generic content type before following redirects;
> - use signed URLs that behave oddly across separate requests.
> 
> Your code gets the content type from the original `HEAD` response:
> 
> ```python
> response = requests.head(url, timeout=timeout)
> content_type = response.headers.get('Content-Type')
> ...
> response = requests.get(url, stream=True, timeout=timeout, allow_redirects=True)
> ```
> 
> So you can download a PDF but classify it based on the wrong response.
> 
> Do a single streamed `GET`, inspect the **final** response headers, and also inspect the actual bytes. Here is a safer replacement.
> 
> ```python
> class DownloadTooLarge(Exception):
>     pass
> 
> 
> def download_url(url, timeout=30, size_limit_mb=20):
>     size_limit_bytes = size_limit_mb * 1024 * 1024
> 
>     with requests.get(
>         url,
>         headers=headers,
>         stream=True,
>         timeout=timeout,
>         allow_redirects=True,
>     ) as response:
>         response.raise_for_status()
> 
>         content_type = response.headers.get("Content-Type", "").lower()
>         content_length = response.headers.get("Content-Length")
> 
>         if content_length is not None:
>             try:
>                 if int(content_length) > size_limit_bytes:
>                     raise DownloadTooLarge(
>                         f"Advertised size exceeds {size_limit_mb} MB"
>                     )
>             except ValueError:
>                 pass
> 
>         data = bytearray()
> 
>         for chunk in response.iter_content(chunk_size=64 * 1024):
>             if not chunk:
>                 continue
> 
>             data.extend(chunk)
> 
>             if len(data) > size_limit_bytes:
>                 raise DownloadTooLarge(
>                     f"Downloaded content exceeds {size_limit_mb} MB"
>                 )
> 
>         return bytes(data), content_type, response.url
> ```
> 
> Then classify based on both MIME type and file signature:
> 
> ```python
> def classify_content(data, content_type):
>     leading = data.lstrip()[:1024].lower()
> 
>     if "application/pdf" in content_type or data.lstrip().startswith(b"%PDF-"):
>         return "pdf"
> 
>     if (
>         "text/html" in content_type
>         or "application/xhtml" in content_type
>         or leading.startswith(b"<!doctype html")
>         or leading.startswith(b"<html")
>     ):
>         return "html"
> 
>     return "unsupported"
> ```
> 
> This matters because many repositories return PDFs as:
> 
> ```text
> application/octet-stream
> ```
> 
> Conversely, an alleged `.pdf` URL may return an HTML paywall, login page, or bot-block page.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. `finder_download()` currently has a type mismatch
> 
> `download_url()` returns:
> 
> ```python
> content.getbuffer()
> ```
> 
> which is a `memoryview`, but later the HTML branch does:
> 
> ```python
> with open(pathprefix + '.html', 'w') as fp:
>     fp.write(content)
> ```
> 
> That expects a Python string, not binary data / a memory view.
> 
> This probably gets swallowed by your broad exception and causes unnecessary fallback to the browser.
> 
> A cleaner version:
> 
> ```python
> async def finder_download(page, url, pathprefix):
>     try:
>         data, content_type, final_url = await asyncio.get_running_loop().run_in_executor(
>             None, download_url, url
>         )
> 
>         kind = classify_content(data, content_type)
> 
>         if kind == "pdf":
>             with open(pathprefix + ".pdf", "wb") as fp:
>                 fp.write(data)
>             return "finder_pdf"
> 
>         if kind == "html":
>             html = data.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
> 
>             # Ignore implausibly short HTML responses, except that a short
>             # bot-block page may still be useful to report.
>             soup = BeautifulSoup(html, "html.parser")
>             if len(soup.get_text(" ", strip=True)) < 100:
>                 raise ValueError("Implausibly small HTML response")
> 
>             with open(pathprefix + ".html", "w", encoding="utf-8") as fp:
>                 fp.write(html)
>             return "finder_html"
> 
>         print(f"Unsupported content type: {content_type}; URL: {final_url}")
>         return "unsupported"
> 
>     except DownloadTooLarge:
>         return "too_big"
> 
>     except (requests.RequestException, ValueError) as exc:
>         # Browser fallback should be only for plausible HTML/JS pages.
>         try:
>             html, content_type = await fetch_url(page, url)
> 
>             if "html" not in content_type.lower():
>                 return "unsupported"
> 
>             with open(pathprefix + ".html", "w", encoding="utf-8") as fp:
>                 fp.write(html)
> 
>             return "finder_html"
> 
>         except Exception as browser_exc:
>             print(f"Could not retrieve {url}: {exc}; browser fallback: {browser_exc}")
>             return "error"
> ```
> 
> And fix this mismatch:
> 
> ```python
> # Current:
> elif nextstep == 'toobig':
> 
> # Should be:
> elif nextstep == 'too_big':
> ```
> 
> Currently your `"too big"` return value does not match either condition, and `explained` can be unbound.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 5. Do not make Google Scholar the primary discovery mechanism
> 
> Scholar scraping is brittle, often rate-limited or CAPTCHA-protected, and its HTML layout changes. It should be an optional convenience source, not your core pipeline.
> 
> Also, this extraction is too broad:
> 
> ```python
> pdf_links = [
>     a['href']
>     for a in soup.find_all('a', href=True)
>     if 'pdf' in a['href'].lower()
> ]
> ```
> 
> Many Scholar PDF links do not have “pdf” in the URL. Conversely, irrelevant links may contain it.
> 
> If you retain Scholar, target its result-side links and resolve relative URLs:
> 
> ```python
> from urllib.parse import urljoin
> 
> pdf_links = [
>     urljoin("https://scholar.google.com", a["href"])
>     for a in soup.select(".gs_or_ggsm a[href]")
> ]
> ```
> 
> But I would prioritize these sources instead.
> 
> ### Better discovery sequence
> 
> Given a citation, title, DOI, or partial reference:
> 
> 1. **Resolve a DOI**
>    - Crossref title search.
> 2. **Query OpenAlex**
>    - `best_oa_location.pdf_url`
>    - all `locations[].pdf_url`
> 3. **Query Unpaywall** for a known DOI
>    - publisher OA PDF;
>    - repository manuscript;
>    - accepted manuscript.
> 4. **Check discipline-specific archives**
>    - arXiv;
>    - PubMed Central / Europe PMC;
>    - SSRN;
>    - RePEc;
>    - bioRxiv / medRxiv;
>    - Zenodo;
>    - OSF;
>    - institutional repositories.
> 5. **Search engine query**
>    - exact title plus `filetype:pdf`;
>    - exact title plus author surname;
>    - DOI plus `pdf`.
> 6. Visit publisher landing pages only if necessary.
> 
> This will generally beat an LLM-directed browsing loop in both success rate and reproducibility.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 6. OpenAlex is especially useful for this
> 
> For a title query, retrieve a handful of likely works and collect OA PDF locations.
> 
> ```python
> def openalex_pdf_candidates(title, per_page=5):
>     response = requests.get(
>         "https://api.openalex.org/works",
>         params={
>             "search": title,
>             "per-page": per_page,
>         },
>         headers=headers,
>         timeout=20,
>     )
>     response.raise_for_status()
> 
>     candidates = []
> 
