**assistant**:
> `async_playwright()` replaces the role currently played by Pyppeteer’s `launch()`. Put it in `main()`, surrounding the batch of documents, and create **one browser/context for the whole batch** rather than launching a new browser for every DOI.
> 
> Playwright is not API-compatible with Pyppeteer, so you should pass a Playwright **browser context** to `finder_pdf`, rather than a Pyppeteer browser.
> 
> ## 1. Replace the import
> 
> ```python
> # Remove:
> # from pyppeteer import launch
> 
> from playwright.async_api import async_playwright
> ```
> 
> Install once:
> 
> ```bash
> pip install playwright
> playwright install chromium
> ```
> 
> If you have normal Google Chrome installed and want to use it instead of Playwright's bundled Chromium, use `channel="chrome"` below.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Create Playwright/browser/context in `main()`
> 
> Here is the relevant structure of `main()` rewritten:
> 
> ```python
> async def main():
>     global found_any
>     found_any = False
> 
>     count = 0
>     verdicts = pd.read_csv(verdict_file.replace(".csv", "-further.csv"))
> 
>     # Do not spoof an old Safari or Chrome version. Let current Chrome identify itself.
>     async with async_playwright() as p:
>         browser = await p.chromium.launch(
>             channel="chrome",   # Remove this line if Chrome is not installed.
>             headless=False,     # Set True once debugging is done.
>             args=["--no-sandbox"],
>         )
> 
>         # A context is an isolated browser session. Set headers / UA / cookies here.
>         context = await browser.new_context(
>             viewport={"width": 1440, "height": 1000},
>             accept_downloads=True,
>         )
> 
>         try:
>             for index, row in verdicts.iterrows():
>                 if row.Priority < priority_limit:
>                     continue
> 
>                 targetpath = os.path.join(
>                     pdfs_dir,
>                     re.sub(r"[^\w.\-]", "_", row.DOI) + ".pdf",
>                 )
>                 statuspath = targetpath[:-4] + ".txt"
> 
>                 if os.path.exists(statuspath):
>                     if os.path.getsize(statuspath) == 0:
>                         os.unlink(statuspath)
>                     elif not is_file_older_than_days(statuspath, refresh_days):
>                         continue
> 
>                 if os.path.exists(targetpath):
>                     continue
> 
>                 print(targetpath)
>                 found_any = True
>                 print(row.DOI)
>                 count += 1
> 
>                 with open(statuspath, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fp:
>                     with contextlib.redirect_stdout(fp):
>                         try:
>                             result, link = await finder.finder_pdf(
>                                 row.DOI,
>                                 context,       # Pass the Playwright context
>                                 targetpath,
>                                 statuspath,
>                             )
>                             print(result)
>                             if link is not None:
>                                 print(link)
> 
>                         except Exception as e:
>                             print(f"Finder error: {type(e).__name__}: {e}")
>                             result, link = "error", None
> 
>                 print([row.DOI, result])
> 
>         finally:
>             await context.close()
>             await browser.close()
> 
>     return count
> ```
> 
> You can remove this Pyppeteer-specific macOS workaround:
> 
> ```python
> if platform.system() == "Darwin":
>     asyncio.set_event_loop_policy(asyncio.DefaultEventLoopPolicy())
> ```
> 
> It was relevant to Pyppeteer’s event-loop behavior, but should not be needed for Playwright.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Change `finder_pdf` to receive a context
> 
> Currently it does this:
> 
> ```python
> async def finder_pdf(document, browser, targetpath, statuspath):
>     page = await browser.newPage()
>     await page.setUserAgent(...)
> ```
> 
> Change it to:
> 
> ```python
> async def finder_pdf(document, context, targetpath, statuspath):
>     page = await context.new_page()
> 
>     try:
>         result, link = await finder_pdf_helper(document, page)
> 
>         try:
>             os.remove("downloaded.html")
>         except FileNotFoundError:
>             pass
> 
>         if result == "found":
>             os.rename("downloaded.pdf", targetpath)
>         else:
>             try:
>                 os.remove("downloaded.pdf")
>             except FileNotFoundError:
>                 pass
> 
>         return result, link
> 
>     finally:
>         try:
>             await page.close()
>         except Exception:
>             pass
> ```
> 
> Do **not** set the user agent on the page. With Playwright, that belongs on the context:
> 
> ```python
> context = await browser.new_context(user_agent="...")
> ```
> 
> But I recommend omitting it altogether. If you use `channel="chrome"`, Playwright will report the real, current Chrome UA. Your existing Safari UA is especially undesirable because it claims Safari while actually running Chromium.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. Update `fetch_url` to Playwright syntax
> 
> Your present Pyppeteer version has the extra-navigation bug discussed before. Use this Playwright version:
> 
> ```python
> async def fetch_url(page, url):
>     response = await page.goto(
>         url,
>         wait_until="domcontentloaded",
>         timeout=30_000,
>     )
> 
>     if response is None:
>         raise RuntimeError(f"No response returned for {url}")
> 
>     # Optional: helps with lightly JavaScript-rendered pages.
>     await page.wait_for_timeout(500)
> 
>     content = await page.content()
>     content_type = response.headers.get("content-type", "")
> 
>     return content, content_type
> ```
> 
> Key translation points:
> 
> | Pyppeteer | Playwright |
> |---|---|
> | `browser.newPage()` | `context.new_page()` |
> | `page.setUserAgent(...)` | `browser.new_context(user_agent=...)` |
> | `page.goto(url, {'timeout': 30000})` | `page.goto(url, timeout=30000)` |
> | `page.waitForNavigation(...)` | Normally do not call it after `goto`; use `wait_until=` |
> | `page.waitForSelector(...)` | `page.wait_for_selector(...)` |
> | `page.close()` | `page.close()` |
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 5. A useful fallback if Chrome is unavailable
> 
> If this fails:
> 
> ```python
> browser = await p.chromium.launch(channel="chrome", ...)
> ```
> 
> because Chrome is not installed or Playwright cannot find it, use Playwright’s managed Chromium:
> 
> ```python
> browser = await p.chromium.launch(
>     headless=False,
>     args=["--no-sandbox"],
> )
> ```
> 
> For debugging the “outdated browser” failures, I would first use:
> 
> ```python
> headless=False
> ```
> 
> You can visually see whether the site is serving:
> 
> - a browser-version warning;
> - a CAPTCHA;
> - a Cloudflare / bot-check interstitial;
> - a consent page;
> - a university proxy/login page;
> - a normal article landing page.
> 
> Once the direct-download / metadata APIs do most of the work, switch to `headless=True` for unattended runs.
